{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And their carcasses you shall detest</b> — to include יַבְחוּשִׁין that are strained. יַבְחוּשִׁין are called מושלונ\"ש in the vernacular. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 11:11). But I am astonished by this, for that species constantly separates from the wine, flies in the air, and lands on the ground, and even from the moment of its flight it is forbidden, as we say in the chapter Elleh Trefot (Ḥullin 67a): Rav Yosef asked: if it flew out into the air of the world, what is the ruling? All the more so does it land on vessels and on the ground, so that one must distance it throughout the entire storeroom all day regularly, and the verse \"any swarming creature that swarms upon the earth\" (Leviticus 11:42) applies to it. Rather, that species is called a יַתוּשׁ (gnat), and it is produced in wine, as we say in the aggadah of the wicked Titus (Leviticus Rabbah 22:3). And the יַבְחוּשִׁים mentioned here are a species that hides in the water, and one scoops it out with a ladle and removes it, from the language of \"one does not stir (בּוֹחֲשִׁין) the sediment on Shabbat\" (Shabbat 156a). And in the Gemara of Zevaḥim, second chapter (22a), it says: whoever may draw water to complete a mikveh may draw water to complete the kiyor basin; to complete a revi'it, one may not draw. And they asked: is this to minimize red יַבְחוּשִׁים? Even according to them as well, for it was taught: Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says, anything whose beginning is from the water — one may immerse in it, for it is a species whose beginning is from the water. And likewise, small white worms are also found regularly in wine dregs, which are created from the coarseness of the wine, and one strains them out. And I saw in his commentary there where he wrote: red יַבְחוּשִׁים are like gnats without wings, in the manner that they grow on the outside of our wine casks. Thin יַבְחוּשִׁים were also created from the water. And perhaps in this he intended a species that has no wings and does not leave the water except by means of straining, and they too are called by that vernacular name by the Rav. But in his commentary on tractate Ḥullin (67a), he wrote: יַבְחוּשִׁים are like thin gnats found in wine cellars. And the general principle is that it is a species that does not swarm upon the earth at all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The parenthetical reference (אין) before the citation from Shabbat 156a appears in the Hebrew source; Ramban likely writes לִשׁוֹן \"אֵין בּוֹחֲשִׁין\" meaning 'the language of: one does not stir,' so I rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:51.645715517Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}